<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Japan</h1><p class="st-meta">1952 CE – present · capital Tokyo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Island state in East Asia, sovereign again since the Allied occupation ended in 1952; a constitutional monarchy under its 1947 constitution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Japan surrendered unconditionally in 1945 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, and was placed under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/occupied-japan">Allied occupation</a> led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1947 it adopted a new constitution emphasising liberal democratic practices. The occupation ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a> in 1952, and Japan joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1956.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The emperor's power is limited to a ceremonial role, while executive power is held by the prime minister and Cabinet, who answer to the bicameral National Diet. The Liberal Democratic Party has been the dominant party since the 1950s. A period of record growth made Japan the world's second-largest economy at that time. It ended in the mid-1990s when an asset price bubble burst, beginning the Lost Decade of economic stagnation and low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>On 11 March 2011 the Tohoku earthquake and the tsunami that followed it triggered the Fukushima nuclear accident. Emperor Akihito abdicated, and on 1 May 2019 his son Naruhito became emperor, beginning the Reiwa era. Sanae Takaichi is prime minister, having taken office after winning the 2025 Liberal Democratic Party leadership election.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/japan.jpg" alt="Japan" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Various · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1952 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tokyo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hirohito, Akihito, Naruhito, Sanae Takaichi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shinto, Buddhism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State of Japan, Nippon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1989&amp;focus=japan" aria-label="Japan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8207 888 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Japan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8527 1144L8527 1144"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8527 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8527 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8527 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tokyo. <a href="/atlas?year=1989&amp;focus=japan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
